   (`--native-sidebar-bg`), so an unconfigured canvas already shows the theme it
   is set to rather than three identical white panels. This rule used to force
   `--surface-panel` here to make the three columns match; matching them meant
   overriding the theme, which is the wrong trade. */

/* Icon-only topbar controls (preview). Square so the icon is optically centred
   rather than sitting in a text button's asymmetric padding. */
/* Edit / delete for the selected canvas module. Positioned by measurement in
   `ModuleToolbar`, so only its appearance is here. Sits above the canvas
   content but below the topbar's menus. */
/* The edit toolbar keeps one look, whatever the canvas is wearing.
   `applyTheme` writes the theme's tokens onto `document.documentElement`, so
   `--surface-panel` / `--border-subtle` / `--text-primary` are global — this
   toolbar was reading them and coming out olive green under Game Boy and
   near-black-on-white-outline under Ink Arcade. It is editor chrome pointing
   *at* the product, not part of it, so it takes the chrome family instead:
   `--fn-bg` is theme-independent, and the two values that had no chrome
   equivalent are pinned to what the default theme sets, which is the Native
   look. `--select-*` is already theme-independent (one fixed
   `--select-color`). */
.module-toolbar {
  --chrome-line: #ececee;
  --chrome-ink: #0f0f10;
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 30;
  display: flex;
  gap: 2px;
  padding: 3px;
  border: 1px solid var(--chrome-line);
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: var(--fn-bg);
  box-shadow: 0 6px 18px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.16), 0 1px 3px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.08);
  /* Measured before it is placed; the inline opacity hides that first frame. */
  transition: opacity var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-out);
}

.module-toolbar-button {
  display: inline-flex;
  gap: 5px;
  align-items: center;
  padding: 4px 8px;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--chrome-ink);
  font-family: inherit;
  font-size: 12px;
  line-height: 16px;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-out);
}

.module-toolbar-button:hover {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--chrome-ink) 7%, transparent);
}

.module-toolbar-button[data-tone="danger"] {
  color: var(--select-strong);
}

.module-toolbar-button[data-tone="danger"]:hover {
  background: var(--select-wash);
}

/* The 8px between the module and its toolbar is a gap the pointer has to cross,
   and crossing it lands on whatever is behind — which used to retarget the
   toolbar out from under the cursor. The grace period in App.tsx covers the
   general case; this closes the gap itself, so the trip is continuous rather
   than merely forgiven.

   A transparent extension rather than a taller toolbar: the visible card keeps
   its size, and `data-placement` already says which side the gap is on, so the
   strip is added below when the toolbar sits above and vice versa. */
.module-toolbar::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  height: 10px;
}

.module-toolbar[data-placement="above"]::after {
  top: 100%;
}

.module-toolbar[data-placement="below"]::after {
  bottom: 100%;
}

/* The `user-select: none` that used to sit here is gone with the attribute that
   carried it. It stopped a drag from highlighting the mock conversation while
   the canvas was a design surface; the canvas runs for real now, so selecting
   text in a reply is something the reader is allowed to do. */

/* The option the canvas toolbar's Edit jumped to. Two flashes, then it stops —
   long enough to find, short enough not to nag. */
@keyframes preset-option-pin {
  0%, 100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 transparent; }
  30%, 70% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--select-ring); }
}

.preset-option-cell[data-pinned="true"] .preset-option {
  animation: preset-option-pin 1.2s var(--ease-out) 2;
}

/* Preview/export completeness prompt. Two ways out, side by side, with
   finishing it yourself on the left because it is the one that keeps the reader
   in the flow they were already in. */
.incomplete-dialog-actions {
  display: flex;
  gap: 8px;
  justify-content: flex-end;
  padding-top: 4px;
}

/* ---- Preferences (`PreferencesDialog`) --------------------------------
   Both settings apply on the click, so the page behind has to stay legible:
   a 30% veil — dark enough to set the sheet apart, light enough that a
   backdrop pick still shows on the canvas behind it. Rows read label-and-help on the left, control on
   the right; backdrops are small canvases; "in force" is a ring and a check,
   never an orange fill. */
.pref-dialog-overlay {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, #0d121c 30%, transparent);
}

.pref-dialog.ui-dialog {
  padding: 22px 22px 20px;
  border-color: rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.08);
  border-radius: 16px;
  box-shadow:
    0 24px 60px -12px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.22),
    0 2px 8px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.06);
  animation: pref-dialog-in 220ms var(--ease-out);
}

@keyframes pref-dialog-in {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translate(-50%, calc(-50% + 8px)) scale(0.97); }
  to { opacity: 1; transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1); }
}

.pref-dialog .ui-dialog-header {
  margin-bottom: 18px;
}

.pref-dialog .ui-dialog-title {
  font-size: 16px;
  letter-spacing: -0.01em;
}

.pref-dialog .ui-dialog-description {
  font-size: 12.5px;
}

.pref-sections {
  display: grid;
  gap: 0;
}

.pref-row {
  display: flex;
  gap: 16px;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  padding: 2px 0 18px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.07);
}

.pref-block {
  display: grid;
  gap: 14px;
  padding-top: 18px;
}

.pref-row-text {
  display: grid;
  gap: 3px;
  min-width: 0;
}

.pref-row-title {
  margin: 0;
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-size: 13px;
  font-weight: 600;
}

.pref-row-hint {
  margin: 0;
  color: var(--text-muted);
  font-size: 12px;
  line-height: 1.45;
}

/* Language: a segmented control. The thumb is one element that slides to
   the index in force, so a change reads as movement, not as two chips
   swapping colour. */
.pref-segmented {
  --pref-seg-w: 64px;
  position: relative;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--pref-seg-count), var(--pref-seg-w));
  flex: none;
  padding: 3px;
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.05);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.04);
}

.pref-segmented-thumb {
  position: absolute;
  top: 3px;
  bottom: 3px;
  left: 3px;
  width: var(--pref-seg-w);
  border-radius: 7px;
  background: #ffffff;
  box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.1), 0 1px 1px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.04);
  transform: translateX(calc(var(--pref-seg-index) * var(--pref-seg-w)));
  transition: transform 260ms cubic-bezier(0.32, 0.72, 0, 1);
}

.pref-segment {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
  height: 28px;
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--text-muted);
  font-family: inherit;
  font-size: 12.5px;
  font-weight: 560;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: color var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-out);
}

.pref-segment:hover {
  color: var(--text-primary);
}

.pref-segment[data-active] {
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-weight: 620;
}

.pref-segment:focus-visible {
  outline: none;
  border-radius: 7px;
  box-shadow: var(--focus-ring);
}

/* Backdrops: two labelled groups of tiles. */
.pref-group {
  display: grid;
  gap: 8px;
}

.pref-group-label {
  color: var(--text-muted);
  font-size: 11px;
  font-weight: 560;
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
}

.pref-tiles {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 10px;
}

.pref-tile {
  display: grid;
  gap: 6px;
  justify-items: center;
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--text-secondary);
  font-family: inherit;
  cursor: pointer;
}

/* The backdrop as a small canvas: its colour and pattern, with a frame on
   it, so a tile says how the canvas will look rather than what colour it is. */
.pref-tile-art {
  position: relative;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
  border-radius: 10px;
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.08);
  transition:
    transform 200ms var(--ease-out),
    box-shadow 200ms var(--ease-out);
}

.pref-tile-frame {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 30% 1fr;
  width: 62%;
  height: 52%;
  overflow: hidden;
  border-radius: 4px;
  background: #ffffff;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.12);
}

.pref-tile-frame-side {
  background: #f1f3f6;
}

.pref-tile-frame-lines {
  display: grid;
  align-content: start;
  gap: 3px;
  padding: 5px;
}

.pref-tile-frame-lines span {
  height: 3px;
  border-radius: 2px;
  background: #e3e6eb;
}

.pref-tile-frame-lines span:last-child {
  width: 60%;
}

.pref-tile-check {
  position: absolute;
  top: 5px;
  right: 5px;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 16px;
  height: 16px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--select-color);
  color: #ffffff;
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px #ffffff;
  opacity: 0;
  transform: scale(0.6);
  transition:
    opacity 160ms var(--ease-out),
    transform 220ms cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1);
}

.pref-tile-label {
  font-size: 12px;
  line-height: 1.2;
  transition: color var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-out);
}

.pref-tile:hover .pref-tile-art {
  transform: translateY(-2px);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.12), 0 6px 14px -6px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.25);
}

.pref-tile:hover .pref-tile-label {
  color: var(--text-primary);
}

.pref-tile[data-active] .pref-tile-art {
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px #ffffff, 0 0 0 3.5px var(--select-color);
}

.pref-tile[data-active] .pref-tile-check {
  opacity: 1;
  transform: scale(1);
}

.pref-tile[data-active] .pref-tile-label {
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-weight: 600;
}

.pref-tile:focus-visible {
  outline: none;
}

.pref-tile:focus-visible .pref-tile-art {
  box-shadow: var(--focus-ring);
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .pref-dialog.ui-dialog,
  .pref-segmented-thumb,
  .pref-tile-art,
  .pref-tile-check {
    animation: none;
    transition: none;
  }
}

/* Canvas zoom: − 100% +, floating at the canvas's top right.
   The same white card as the rail and the canvas panel, at their gap from the
   window's top, so it reads as one more piece of the editor's furniture rather
   than as something drawn on the canvas. It sits in the canvas's right gutter
   edge — `--canvas-pad-right` is the room the panel takes — so it follows the
   panel open and folded instead of sliding under it. */
.canvas-zoom-bar {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: var(--z-shell-chrome);
  top: var(--fn-float-gap);
  right: var(--canvas-pad-right, 24px);
  transition: right var(--motion-rail-out) var(--ease-out);
}

.zoom-control {
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 2px;
  height: 32px;
  padding: 2px;
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: var(--fn-bg);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-md), 0 0 0 1px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.06);
}

.zoom-step,
.zoom-trigger {
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--text-secondary);
  font-family: inherit;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition:
    background var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-out),
    color var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-out);
}

.zoom-step {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 28px;
  height: 28px;
  padding: 0;
}

.zoom-step:hover:not(:disabled),
.zoom-trigger:hover,
.zoom-trigger[aria-expanded="true"] {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--text-primary) 6%, transparent);
  color: var(--text-primary);
}

.zoom-step:disabled {
  color: var(--text-muted);
  opacity: 0.45;
  cursor: default;
}

.zoom-trigger {
  display: inline-flex;
  gap: 2px;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  height: 28px;
  padding: 0 4px 0 8px;
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-size: 12px;
  font-weight: 500;
  line-height: 20px;
}

.zoom-trigger svg {
  color: var(--text-muted);
}

.zoom-trigger-value {
  /* Tabular so the control does not resize as the number changes width. */
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  min-width: 34px;
  text-align: center;
}

.zoom-menu {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 40;
  top: calc(100% + 8px);
  right: 0;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 6px;
  width: 208px;
  padding: 8px;
  border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: var(--surface-panel);
  box-shadow: 0 12px 32px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.14), 0 2px 6px rgba(13, 18, 28, 0.06);
}

.zoom-menu-field {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 5px 8px;
  border: 1px solid var(--select-line);
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: var(--surface-panel);
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-family: inherit;
  font-size: 13px;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  line-height: 20px;
  outline: 0;
}

.zoom-menu-field::selection {
  background: var(--select-fill);
}

.zoom-menu-items {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  padding-top: 6px;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
}

.zoom-menu-item {
  display: flex;
  gap: 8px;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  padding: 6px 8px;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-family: inherit;
  font-size: 13px;
  line-height: 20px;
  text-align: left;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.zoom-menu-item:hover:not(:disabled) {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--text-primary) 6%, transparent);
}

.zoom-menu-item:disabled {
  color: var(--text-muted);
  cursor: default;
}

.zoom-menu-item-hint {
  padding: 1px 6px;
  border-radius: 4px;
  background: var(--surface-inset);
  color: var(--text-secondary);
  font-family: inherit;
  font-size: 11px;
  line-height: 16px;
}

.topbar-icon-button {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 32px;
  height: 32px;
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: var(--fn-r-sm);
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--text-primary);
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-out);
}

.topbar-icon-button:hover {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--text-primary) 6%, transparent);
}

/* The panel stacks a variable number of blocks: some destinations lead with a
   group list or with the style cards, then the header, then the body.
   `.preset-panel` declares two explicit grid rows, so a third child lands on an
   implicit one — which put the header on the `minmax(0, 1fr)` track and the
   body on an `auto` track. With a tall body the 1fr resolved to 0px and the
   header's 20px title overflowed onto the content below it. Theme and Structure
   only escaped it because their bodies happened to be shorter.
   Flex column sizes each block from its own content and lets only the body
   flex, so the number of leading blocks stops mattering. */
.preset-panel:is([data-nav-section="structure"], [data-nav-section="components"], [data-nav-section="layers"]) {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

.preset-panel:is([data-nav-section="structure"], [data-nav-section="components"], [data-nav-section="layers"])
  > :is(.preset-style-cards, .preset-panel-header, .layers-panel-head) {
  flex: none;
}

/* Structure and Components nest their options inside the group list, so the
   list *is* the body — it takes the leftover height and scrolls. It used to be
   `flex: none` alongside the style cards, which was right when the options
   lived in a separate `.preset-panel-body` below it. Left that way, a group
   with many options grew the list past the bottom of the window and the groups
   below it became unreachable: measured 1803px of list in an 848px rail. */
.preset-panel:is([data-nav-section="structure"], [data-nav-section="components"]) > .group-list {
  flex: 1;
  min-height: 0;
  overflow-y: auto;
  padding-top: 8px;
  scrollbar-width: none;
}

.preset-panel:is([data-nav-section="structure"], [data-nav-section="components"])
  > .group-list::-webkit-scrollbar {
  width: 0;
  height: 0;
  display: none;
}


.preset-panel:is([data-nav-section="structure"], [data-nav-section="components"], [data-nav-section="layers"])
  > .preset-panel-body {
  flex: 1;
  min-height: 0;
}

/* ── Studio (插画风) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Spec: docs/STUDIO_ILLUSTRATED_SPEC.md, extracted from Frame 60.

   Three corrections over the first pass, all of them things the reference says
   plainly once you look at it at 4x:

   1. The columns are solid blocks butted against each other, divided by one
      black line — not cards with their own borders floating on a canvas. Two
      adjacent borders is the doubled line that made it read as a wireframe.
   2. The icons' own closed outlines are filled yellow. There is no container
      box: the magnifier's lens *is* the yellow disc. The first pass put a
      yellow square behind a black glyph, which is the same idea inside out.
   3. Containers exist in exactly two places — the send key and the file-type
      badge — and a yellow container flips its glyph to solid black.

   Scoped to `.preview-frame`: this styles the product being designed, not the
   editor drawing it. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] {
  /* The two column fills. `ThemeTokens.surface` has no slot for them (it runs
     canvas/panel/raised/inset/hover/disabled), and adding one would touch all
     27 themes — so they live here, the only raw hex in the branch. */
  /* Three fills `ThemeTokens.surface` has no slot for — the two side columns
     and the file-type badge. They default to the first palette's values and are
     overridden per palette below, keyed on `data-theme-preset`, which the frame
     already carries. That is why they are not in the schema: adding slots would
     touch all 30 themes to serve four, and these are "one palette of one
     style's" colours rather than anything general.

     Constraint for any new palette: the side columns get no text colour of
     their own, so they inherit `--text-primary`. A column fill therefore has to
     stay light enough for that ink — which is why none of the light palettes
     uses a deep saturated column. */
  --studio-sidebar-fill: #9871fe;
  --studio-panel-fill: #c9b5fe;
  --studio-category-fill: #4ecda4;
  /* The thinking block's ink: the bold "Thinking" and its body share one
     colour, so it is a value rather than a pair of overrides. Per palette like
     the fills above — Ink Arcade cannot use a mid violet on a near-black
     canvas. */
  --studio-thinking-ink: #695993;
  /* The card that sits behind the composer, offset down and to the right. */
  --studio-backing-fill: #c9b5fe;
  --studio-backing-offset: 6px;
  /* Two line weights, and the split is structure against content.
     It started at 3px on edges and 2px on chips — two weights of the *same*
     line, which is the thing worth avoiding, and at these corner radii the
     heavier one closed the corners up. Then one weight for everything, at 2px,
     which read as heavy once every card, bubble, pill and tile inside the
     frame was carrying it: a column of 2px rectangles is a lot of ink.

     So the split is by what the line is *for*. The frame and the dividers
     between the three columns hold the layout together and stay at 2px —
     they are the only lines that have to read from across the view. Everything
     inside a column is content and goes to 1.5px, which at these sizes is
     visibly lighter without ever dropping to a hairline.

     Both are real weights rather than one weight and a tint: this style draws
     with ink, and a grey 2px line would read as a disabled 2px line.

     The content weight is 1px and not the 1.5px asked for, because Chrome
     floors fractional border widths to whole CSS pixels — `border-width: 1.5px`
     computes to `1px`, at devicePixelRatio 2 as well as 1, and paints two
     device pixels rather than three. (`outline-width` floors too; only a
     box-shadow spread keeps the fraction, and a shadow takes no layout space,
     which would take the height formula in §4 with it.) So 1px is what 1.5px
     renders as, and declaring it is the stylesheet saying what happens. */
  --studio-edge-frame: 2px;
  --studio-edge: 1px;

  /* ---- The token layer (spec §1–§8) ------------------------------------
     Defined once here so everything below is a *reference* rather than a
     value. The point of the layer is that a new component cannot invent a
     height or a radius — there is nothing to invent from.

     `--st-ink` follows the theme rather than being literally black: Ink Arcade
     is the dark palette of this same style, and there the line is near-white.
     "One ink" means one *value per palette*, not one hex. */
  --st-ink: var(--border-strong);
  --st-ink-quiet: color-mix(in srgb, var(--text-primary) 55%, transparent);

  /* Vertical padding, four steps. These are the only legal values, because
     they are what makes the heights below land on the 4px grid. */
  /* Each step went up by 1 when the content line went down by 1, so that every
     derived height stays exactly where it was: H = lineBox + 2·padY + 2·border,
     and 2·border just lost 2px. The heights are the contract — 36 for a row,
     48 for a single-line bubble, both read off the screen and agreed — so the
     padding is what absorbs a change in line weight, not the height. */
  --st-pad-tight: 5px;
  --st-pad-snug: 7px;
  --st-pad-base: 9px;
  --st-pad-loose: 11px;

  /* Heights, computed: lineBox + 2×padY + 2×edge. Written out rather than
     `calc()`ed so they can be read at a glance and asserted against.
       label 16 + 8  + 4 = 28
       row   20 + 12 + 4 = 36
       row   20 + 16 + 4 = 40
       row   20 + 20 + 4 = 44 */
  --st-h-chip: 28px;
  --st-h-row: 36px;
  --st-h-ctl: 40px;
  --st-h-action: 44px;

  /* Type: five line boxes and four weights are this style's own; the *sizes*
     are not. They were 20/16/15/14/11 from the spec, and that is how 漫画风
     ended up the only style with 15px copy and 11px labels — a second scale
     beside the shared one, differing by a step in three places for no reason
     anyone had decided.
     So the five names stay (the rules that read them are worth keeping legible)
     and each points at the shared token. `display` keeps a size of its own:
     it is the canvas title, which is where a style is allowed to speak. */
  --st-t-display: 20px;
  --st-lh-display: 28px;
  --st-t-title: var(--preview-title-size);
  --st-lh-title: 24px;
  --st-t-body: var(--preview-copy-size);
  --st-lh-body: 24px;
  --st-t-row: var(--preview-copy-size);
  --st-lh-row: 20px;
  --st-t-label: var(--preview-meta-size);
  --st-lh-label: 16px;

  /* Spacing. Rows sit 4px apart, not 8: a 36px row plus an 8px gap reads as a
     stack of cards rather than as a list. */
  --st-gap-inline-tight: 6px;
  --st-gap-inline: 8px;
  --st-gap-stack-tight: 4px;
  --st-gap-stack: 8px;
  --st-gap-stack-loose: 16px;
  --st-gap-inset: 10px;
  --st-gap-inset-wide: 14px;

  /* The only shadow. Zero blur, and only on things that can be pressed. */
  --st-shadow-press: 0 3px 0 0 var(--st-ink);

  /* The radius scale, taken down a second tier.
     It started at 12/20 — a corner wider than the 2px line drawing it — and
     halving fixed the bubble but left the centre column still reading as
     rounded, because the largest surfaces live there: the composer shell, the
     artifact card and the error block were all on the 12px tier at once, so
     three big soft rectangles stacked down the middle of the view.
     Now the step *is* the line weight: one tier = 2px = one line. That gives a
     scale whose arithmetic matches the only other measurement in the style,
     and a chip whose corner is exactly the relief of its own outline. */
  --st-r-chip: 2px;
  --st-r-row: 4px;
  --st-r-card: 6px;
  --st-r-panel: 8px;

  /* The size of a glyph that *is* a control — the sidebar's search and
     collapse buttons, the new-task mark, the panel float. They are passed
     `size={15}` by components every style shares, and at 15px a glyph drawn as
     a filled shape with a 2px outline puts its line on a seventh of its own
     width; the new-task icon's folded corner closes up entirely.

     Only these. The composer's controls and the message actions answer to the
     sizes their own components pass. */
  --st-i-chrome: 20px;

  /* The project's own radius tokens point at the tiers, so every component
     follows without a rule of its own. Mapped by the height each tier serves,
     which is the whole reason the scale is derived rather than chosen. */
  --fn-r-xs: var(--st-r-chip);
  --fn-r-sm: var(--st-r-row);
  --fn-r-md: var(--st-r-card);
  --fn-r-lg: var(--st-r-card);
  --fn-r-xl: var(--st-r-panel);
  --radius-sm: var(--st-r-row);
  --radius-md: var(--st-r-card);
  --radius-lg: var(--st-r-card);
  --radius: var(--st-r-card);

  /* Weights, as the four the scale allows. The components read these, so a
     450 cannot creep back in through a shared rule. */
  --weight-eyebrow: 700;
  --weight-title: 800;
  --weight-strong: 700;
  --weight-body: 500;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.session-brand-title, .sidebar-title) {
  font-weight: 800;
}

/* ---- The sidebar's type and its selected row --------------------------
   Three things were wrong here and only one of them was the radius.

   The rows and the date headings were *not* black: they resolved to #1E1E1E
   and #8B7BB8 respectively, because they carry their own colours rather than
   inheriting `--text-primary` — which is why the wordmark beside them was the
   only truly black thing in the column. Both are content in this style, so
   both take the ink.

   And the selected row was a violet pill with no outline. In a style whose
   entire language is "a white surface inside a black line", the one marked row
   was the one thing not drawn that way. The mark is `.session-item-bg`, a
   dedicated span behind the label — not the row element, which is why earlier
   rules aimed at `.session-item` never landed: that class sits on the button
   *inside* the shell, and the shell is `.session-item-shell[data-active]`. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-sidebar :is(.session-item, .session-item-shell) {
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-size: var(--st-t-row);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-row);
  font-weight: 600;
}

/* The row is a computed height, not a content-sized one. 36px = 20 line box +
   6 padding either side + 2 border either side. Both the shell and the button
   inside it, or the pill and the label disagree by a pixel or two and the line
   reads as misaligned. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-sidebar
  :is(.session-item-shell, .session-item, .session-item-bg) {
  min-height: var(--st-h-row);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-list .session-group {
  gap: var(--st-gap-stack-tight);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-group > h4 {
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-size: var(--st-t-label);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-label);
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-brand-name {
  font-size: var(--st-t-display);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-display);
  font-weight: 800;
}

/* Every row's pill takes the row radius, not just the marked one.
   The unmarked pills are transparent, so their 12px corner is invisible at
   rest — but they are what the hover fill paints, so hovering an unselected
   row showed a 12px-rounded block directly above a 4px selected one. Caught by
   the radius sweep, which reads geometry rather than what is currently
   visible. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-item-bg {
  border-radius: var(--st-r-row);
  /* The pill is the row's box, not an inset inside it.
     Generically it sits `inset: 2px` from the shell, which leaves it 32px tall
     in a 36px row — but the height rule above gives it `min-height: 36px`, and
     an absolutely positioned box that cannot shrink grows downward from its
     top inset. So the pill ended up 2px below the button and 2px past its
     bottom, and the label — correctly centred in the *button* — read as 6px
     from the top and 10px from the bottom of the shape actually drawn around
     it. Measured: button 425..461, pill 427..463.
     Zeroing the block inset makes the two boxes the same box. The inline bleed
     stays: that is what lets the pill run past the list's padding. */
  inset-block: 0;
}

/* The marked row: white inside the line, like every other surface here. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-item-shell[data-active="true"] .session-item-bg {
  background: var(--surface-panel);
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--st-ink);
  border-radius: var(--st-r-row);
}

/* ---- Controls: the computed heights (spec §4) -------------------------
   Every control with a background lands on one of four heights. Written as
   `min-height` rather than `height` so a control whose label wraps grows
   instead of clipping — the height is a floor the grid guarantees, not a cage.

   The pairing of height to padding is fixed: a 40px control padded at 6px
   would have a 24px line box, which is not a size in the scale. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.primary-button, .secondary-button) {
  min-height: var(--st-h-ctl);
  padding: var(--st-pad-base) var(--st-gap-inset-wide);
  border-radius: var(--st-r-card);
  font-size: var(--st-t-row);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-row);
  font-weight: 600;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.session-new, .send-button) {
  min-height: var(--st-h-action);
  font-size: var(--st-t-row);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-row);
  font-weight: 600;
}

/* The send key is one of the two circles in the style; the other is the
   avatar. Everything else takes a tier from the radius scale. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .send-button {
  min-width: var(--st-h-action);
  border-radius: var(--radius-full);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(
  .session-search-trigger,
  .session-collapse,
  .rail-icon-btn,
  .tool-style-badge
) {
  min-height: var(--st-h-chip);
  min-width: var(--st-h-chip);
  border-radius: var(--st-r-chip);
}

/* ---- States (spec §10) ------------------------------------------------
   The line never changes; the fill does. Selected is the exception and it
   changes both, because in this style being selected means *becoming an
   object* — gaining an outline — rather than getting a darker wash. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.session-item, .primary-button, .secondary-button, .session-new):hover:not(:disabled) {
  background-color: var(--surface-hover);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.primary-button, .secondary-button, .session-new, .send-button):disabled {
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--st-ink) 40%, transparent);
  color: var(--st-ink-quiet);
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* A ring rather than a colour change, offset so it reads as a separate mark
   from the 2px outline it sits outside. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(button, a, textarea, input):focus-visible {
  outline: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--accent);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* Placeholders are the one place `--ink-quiet` is allowed: a pure-black
   placeholder is read as content already typed. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(textarea, input)::placeholder {
  color: var(--st-ink-quiet);
}

/* ---- Sweep: the values the audit found off-scale ----------------------
   Run against the live canvas with the checklist in the spec (§12), these are
   what it named. Listed by selector rather than blanketed with a `*` rule: a
   universal override would also hit the things that are deliberately
   different, and there would be no record of what was actually wrong.

   Two kinds of offender:

   1px borders. This style has one line weight, so a 1px border is either a
   leftover hairline (drop it) or a line that should be 2px. Each of these is
   the former: the row's line belongs to its pill, the inline message actions
   and the thinking block carry no line at all (§6).

   13px / 400 / 450. Sizes and weights outside the five-and-four the scale
   allows. 450 in particular is weight used as a nudge dial. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(
  .session-item,
  .session-new,
  .message-action-icon,
  .reasoning-block
) {
  border-width: 0;
}

/* The two speaker avatars carry no ring.
   They had one because the base `.msg-avatar` draws `1px solid
   var(--border-strong)` and this style had no rule of its own — measured black
   on both roles — so a 28px circle picked up a hard black edge that nothing in
   the reference has. The fill stays: the face is a black-line glyph on a
   coloured disc, and the disc is what tells the two roles apart. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .msg-avatar {
  border: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(
  .session-item-label,
  .message-role,
  .assistant-turn-label,
  .tool-file-row-meta,
  .session-footer
) {
  font-size: var(--st-t-label);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-label);
  font-weight: 700;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-item-label {
  /* The row's own label reads at row size, not label size — it is content. */
  font-size: var(--st-t-row);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-row);
  font-weight: 600;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(
  .tool-title,
  .tool-file-row-status,
  .tool-title-file
) {
  font-size: var(--st-t-row);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-row);
  font-weight: 600;
}

/* The bubble measured 52px against the 48 the formula gives: its paragraph
   brings a line-height and a margin of its own, so the text box was 4px taller
   than the 24px line box the padding was computed for. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .msg-surface > * {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: var(--st-t-body);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-body);
}

/* ---- The base, so legal values are what you inherit -------------------
   The first sweep named selectors one at a time and the audit kept finding
   more, because the offenders were not a list — they were everything the style
   branch had not reached yet. Elements with no class of their own were
   inheriting the app's defaults (13px, weight 450) straight through.

   So the scale goes on the frame and on the bare elements, and inheritance
   carries it. Anything with an opinion of its own still overrides; the base
   only decides what an element gets when nobody said otherwise. 450 and 640
   cannot be inherited from here because they are not written here. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] {
  font-size: var(--st-t-body);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-body);
  font-weight: var(--weight-body);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(p, li, td) {
  font-size: var(--st-t-body);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-body);
  font-weight: var(--weight-body);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(strong, b) {
  font-weight: 700;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(small, figcaption) {
  font-size: var(--st-t-label);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-label);
  font-weight: 500;
}

/* ---- The remaining chrome --------------------------------------------
   Cards, controls and the composer, which had radii off the tier set (14, 16,
   18), hairline borders, and a soft ambient shadow — all three of the things
   §5/§6/§7 replace. Radii are assigned by what the thing *is*, not by rounding
   the number it had: a chip goes to the chip tier even when it was carrying 14.

   `.artifact-launch-kind` at 12px is a kind marker, so it takes the label size;
   `.ui-button` is content-bearing, so it takes the row size. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-shell {
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--st-ink);
  border-radius: var(--st-r-panel);
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* The artifact card keeps its line — it is a card, and this is the style's one
   line weight. The generated-image and error variants drop theirs in the rule
   further down: an image already ends where it ends, and an error is a sentence
   rather than a container. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-inline {
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--st-ink);
  border-radius: var(--st-r-panel);
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* Two of these do not get the line, and the reason is the same for both: it was
   an outline around something that already had an edge.

   The generated image brings its own rectangle — the photograph ends where it
   ends — so a card outline around it draws a second frame a few pixels outside
   the first, with a strip of background caught between them. The error block is
   a sentence with an icon, not a card; boxing it says "this is a container"
   about something that holds nothing.

   The sweep put the line on both because it worked from "every border is 2px or
   0" and read anything card-shaped as wanting 2px. The rule is a floor on the
   line's *weight*, not an instruction to draw one. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.generated-image-inline, .error-item) {
  border: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-inline-icon {
  border-radius: var(--st-r-chip);
}

/* The artifact tile, to the values specified.
   A 20px corner and a 3px line, both off the scales in §5 and §6 — on purpose.
   This is an icon *tile*: the container and the glyph are one piece of artwork,
   drawn to its own proportions the way an app icon is, so its corner answers to
   the drawing inside it rather than to the row it sits in. §9 records the
   exception and the scale guard exempts this selector by name, so it stays one
   selector wide instead of becoming a hole in the rule. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-launch-card .artifact-inline-icon {
  /* Both of the artwork's own numbers were read back as too heavy once drawn,
     and both rejoined the scale: 20px was a corner eating nearly half of a
     44px side (§5 caps it at 44/6), and 3px was a line a pixel thicker than
     every other line in the style — on a 44px tile that is the widest stroke
     in the view. Nothing here is off-scale now, which is why the guard no
     longer carries an exemption for it. */
  border-radius: var(--st-r-card);
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--st-ink);
  background: var(--studio-category-fill);
}

/* ---- Chrome icons, at 20px rather than 15 -----------------------------
   The glyphs are passed `size={15}` in the components, which is the density
   the plainer styles want. This style draws them as filled shapes with a 2px
   outline and, in the new-task icon's case, a folded corner and a bevel — at
   15px the outline lands near a pixel and the detail closes up.

   Set on the wrappers rather than by changing the prop: the prop is shared by
   every style, and the two spans it feeds are already the per-style switch. The
   svg then fills its wrapper, so one number moves both the box and the drawing
   and they cannot disagree.

   20 fits the 28px chip the rail buttons are: 4px of air on each side. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(
  .native-rail-icon,
  .legacy-rail-icon,
  .comic-new-icon,
  .default-new-icon
) {
  width: var(--st-i-chrome);
  height: var(--st-i-chrome);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(
  .native-rail-icon,
  .legacy-rail-icon,
  .comic-new-icon,
  .default-new-icon
) > svg {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}

/* The two rail buttons that hold a glyph with no wrapper span: the sidebar's
   collapse control and the panel float. Scoped to `.rail-icon-btn` and nothing
   else — the composer's controls, the message actions, the tool status glyph
   and the error icon keep the size their components pass. They were resized
   once, on the reasoning that five icon sizes in one frame is four too many;
   that is true and still not what was asked for. If the rest should move, it
   should move as its own decision. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .rail-icon-btn > svg {
  width: var(--st-i-chrome);
  height: var(--st-i-chrome);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .ui-button {
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--st-ink);
  border-radius: var(--st-r-row);
  font-size: var(--st-t-row);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-row);
  font-weight: 600;
}

/* The open-with control reads as a fill, not as an outline.
   It sits inside a card that now has no line of its own, so an outlined control
   there was the only line in the row and read as the heavier of the two
   elements — louder than the file name it belongs to. A tinted fill says
   "pressable" without drawing a third rectangle.

   `--surface-inset` rather than a literal lavender: it is the palette's own
   quiet fill, so this follows all four of the style's colourways instead of
   being violet in the one it was picked from. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-action-open {
  border: 0;
  border-radius: var(--st-r-row);
  background: var(--surface-inset);
  font-size: var(--st-t-row);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-row);
  /* 500, not 400: this style's weight scale has four steps and 500 is the
     lightest — see `studioScale.test.ts`. Against the file name's 700 it still
     reads as unbolded. */
  font-weight: 500;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-launch-kind {
  font-size: var(--st-t-label);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-label);
  font-weight: 500;
}

/* ---- Depth (spec §7) --------------------------------------------------
   One value, zero blur, and only on what can be pressed.

   "Only on what can be pressed" is the whole rule, and this rule broke it: the
   list included `.tool-card`, `.utility-card`, `.artifact-frame` and
   `.composer-frame`, none of which are buttons. A zero-blur offset shadow under
   a full-width panel does not read as depth — it reads as a 3px black rule
   drawn across the column, which is what the reader asked about. Depth needs an
   edge small enough to see around; on a panel that spans the view there is no
   around. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.primary-button, .secondary-button) {
  box-shadow: var(--st-shadow-press);
}

/* `.session-new` came off this list for the second half of the same rule: a
   zero-blur offset shadow needs a filled face in front of it, or there is
   nothing for it to be the underside *of*. That button draws no background and
   no border here, so the shadow was the only thing painted — a 3px black arc
   tracing a 6px corner, floating under an invisible button. Its icon and label
   are the affordance; it does not need a plinth. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-new {
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* The panels it was taken off, said explicitly: several of them are given a
   shadow by a generic rule further up, so removing them from the list above
   would otherwise hand them an ambient blurred one instead. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(
  .tool-card,
  .utility-card,
  .artifact-frame,
  .composer-frame,
  .chat-frame
) {
  box-shadow: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.primary-button, .secondary-button, .session-new, .send-button):active:not(:disabled) {
  transform: translateY(3px);
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* ---- Message bubbles --------------------------------------------------
   The same 2px line and the larger of the two radii. They were at 3px and
   20px — a corner wider than the line drawing it. */
/* The bubble is a sentence, not a card, so it takes the tighter of the two
   multi-line insets — 10/14 rather than the card's 12/14. It was laid out at
   card padding, which is why it read as too tall. Single line lands at
   24 + 20 + 4 = 48px, and 48/8 gives the 6px corner. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .msg-surface {
  padding: var(--st-pad-loose) var(--st-gap-inset-wide);
  border-width: var(--studio-edge);
  border-color: var(--st-ink);
  border-radius: var(--st-r-card);
  /* The ink, explicitly: the bubble carries its own colour rather than
     inheriting, and resolved to #1E1E1E — near-black, which beside a pure
     black outline reads as a slightly faded text. */
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-size: var(--st-t-body);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-body);
  font-weight: 500;
  /* Flat: thickness is this style's cue for "you can press this", and a bubble
     is not pressable. */
  box-shadow: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(h1, h2, h3, h4) {
  font-weight: 500;
}

/* The padding above resolved to 12px, not the 10 it asks for: the generic
   `.preview-frame .message-item[data-role="user"] .msg-surface` carries one
   more class than a style branch does, so it outranked it. Restated at the
   same shape for both lanes. Measured, not assumed — the bubble was 52px
   against the formula's 48, and 24 + 12 + 12 + 2 + 2 is exactly 52. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .message-item[data-role="user"] .msg-surface,
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .message-bubble[data-role="assistant"] .msg-surface,
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .assistant-lane .msg-surface {
  padding: var(--st-pad-loose) var(--st-gap-inset-wide);
}

/* ---- The last of the off-scale labels ---------------------------------
   Control labels that set their own 12/13px, and the model picker's hairline.
   Each sits one level inside something already corrected, which is why the
   rule on the parent did not reach them: `font-size` on a button does not
   override a span inside it that declares its own. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(
  .permission-mode-trigger,
  .budget-mode-trigger,
  .provider-model-summary
) :is(span, em, strong) {
  font-size: var(--st-t-row);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-row);
  font-weight: 600;
  font-style: normal;
}

/* `.artifact-action-open` was in the list above and is split out for its
   weight alone. Its inner span is the word "打开" / "Open with", which reads at
   body weight in every style — see the note on `.artifact-launch-kind`. The
   size correction is the same, which is why it was grouped there. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-action-open :is(span, em, strong) {
  font-size: var(--st-t-row);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-row);
  font-weight: 500;
  font-style: normal;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .provider-model-picker {
  border-width: 0;
}

/* The launch card's title asks for 640 — a weight used as a dial, between the
   two steps the scale allows. It rounds up: this is the card's title, and 700
   is the title weight. Three classes deep, so it needs the same reach. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-launch-card .artifact-inline-body strong {
  font-weight: 700;
}

/* ---- Structure: three solid blocks, one black line between them ---------
   The frame is the single rounded black rectangle; the columns inside it are
   square and meet edge to edge. Clipping to the frame is what lets the columns
   be square without poking out of its corners. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] {
  border: var(--studio-edge-frame) solid var(--border-subtle);
  border-radius: var(--fn-r-xl);
  overflow: hidden;
  background: var(--surface-canvas);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-sidebar {
  background: var(--studio-sidebar-fill);
  /* No edge of its own. The column meets its neighbour at the resize handle,
     which is the one thing standing between them — an edge here plus a filled
     handle drew two black lines with a white channel between them, which is
     the thick band this used to show. */
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* Standing rule (see the Retro branch for the full statement): the right panel
   is not a surface of its own — no fill, no frame, no ornament. Only the left
   column keeps this style's panel chrome. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .right-panel {
  background: transparent;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* One black line between the blocks, at the same weight as the frame's own
   edge. The reference has exactly one, which is why this went through a round
   of drawing two: the strip was filled *and* its `::after` drew inside it. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] {
  --divider-w: var(--studio-edge-frame);
  --divider-c: var(--border-subtle);
}

/* ---- Outlines: a short list, and it stays short ------------------------
   Only the objects that are *cards* in their own right. Everything struck from
   this list was drawing a second line around something that already had one, or
   around something that reads as text rather than as an object:

   - `.message-bubble` wraps the whole message row, not just the bubble, so an
     outline here boxed the row — including the empty half beside a
     right-aligned user bubble.
   - `.composer-frame` is the outer holder; the field inside it is the card the
     reader sees. Outlining both drew a box inside a box.
   - `.session-item` selected: the sidebar already marks it with a filled
     surface, so the outline was a third marker on one row.
   - `.tool-card`: it sits *inside* the conversation, already indented under the
     reasoning rule and carrying its own status colour and progress bar. A heavy
     frame around that made one step of a run louder than the answer it belongs
     to. Removed from every style's framing for the same reason, not just this
     one — see the note in each branch.
   - `.msg-avatar`: a 28px disc with a black-line face on it. The ring was a
     third edge on a glyph that is already all edges, and it is where the
     border-zeroing rule further up this branch was being lost — this list is
     later in the file, so it won on source order. Zeroed there, struck here. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(
  .utility-card,
  .artifact-frame
) {
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--border-subtle);
}

/* This was the second copy of the same mistake, and a 4px one — two shadow
   values in a style that has one. Dropped rather than retuned: these are
   panels. */

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.primary-button, .secondary-button) {
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--border-strong);
  box-shadow: 0 3px 0 0 var(--border-strong);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.primary-button, .secondary-button):active:not(:disabled) {
  transform: translateY(3px);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 var(--border-strong);
}

/* ---- The reader's own turn --------------------------------------------
   White, 3px black, 20px. `.message-bubble` wraps the whole row rather than the
   bubble, so the edge goes on `.msg-surface` — outlining the wrapper boxes the
   empty half of the row beside a right-aligned turn. */
/* The conversation area is the canvas, not a white card.
   `.chat-frame` shares a rule with `.utility-card` and takes
   `--surface-panel`, which is white in every Comic palette — so the centre
   column came out white above the composer and the specified canvas colour
   only showed in the strip below it. The column is one surface here; the cards
   *on* it (tool calls, output) keep their own panel fill. */
/* Studio's explicit canvas fill is gone: `.preview-frame .chat-frame` is
   transparent for every style now, so the conversation shows the frame's canvas
   without each branch restating it. */

/* The output panel is part of the right column, not a card sitting in it.
   `.artifact-frame` also carries `.utility-card`, so it was picking up that
   rule's white fill *and* this style's 3px outline and offset shadow — a white
   box with a black edge floating on the violet column.

   Scoped by `data-output-surface`, which the component already sets from
   `project.output.surface`, rather than by descending from `.right-panel`: the
   same card can be sent to the overlay instead, and there it *is* a card and
   should keep its edge. Tool cards in the conversation are untouched either
   way — they share the classes but never this attribute. */
/* In the right column the output panel *is* the column, not a card sitting in
   it. Every style framed it — `.artifact-frame` also carries `.utility-card`,
   so it picked up that rule's fill, plus whatever edge and decoration the style
   adds to cards — and the result was a bordered box floating on the column it
   fills. Studio had this fixed; the rest did not, so it moves out of the style
   branch and applies to all of them.

   Scoped by `data-output-surface`, which the component sets from
   `project.output.surface`, rather than by descending from `.right-panel`: the
   same card can be sent to the overlay, and *there* it is a card and keeps its
   edge. `background` is the shorthand on purpose — it resets
   `background-image` too, which is what carries Game's corner ornaments. */
.preview-frame .artifact-frame[data-output-surface="right-panel"] {
  background: transparent;
  border: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* Zine draws its torn top edge with a pseudo-element, which outlives the
   border it belonged to. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="zine"] .artifact-frame[data-output-surface="right-panel"]::before {
  display: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .message-bubble {
  background: transparent;
  border: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .message-bubble[data-role="user"] .msg-surface {
  background: var(--surface-panel);
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--border-subtle);
  border-radius: var(--fn-r-xl);
}

/* ---- Thinking block ---------------------------------------------------
   One ink *and* one size for both halves. The title used to be
   `--text-primary` and the body `--text-muted` at another size, which is two
   voices where the spec asks for one. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .reasoning-block {
  --reasoning-type-color: var(--studio-thinking-ink);
  --reasoning-type-weight: 700;
}

/* Colour and weight come from `--reasoning-type-*` above; this rule set them a
   second time, on the body alone. */

/* ---- Composer, with a card behind it ----------------------------------
   Two stacked shadows rather than a pseudo-element, which is what makes the
   backing card work at all: a `::before` with `z-index: -1` paints *below the
   parent's own background*, so the composer's white would have covered it. A
   shadow is painted outside the border box and cannot be covered.

   First the violet fill offset by 6px, then the same offset again with a 3px
   spread in black — later shadows paint behind earlier ones, so the black reads
   as a 3px outline around the violet rather than over it. Both inherit the
   element's `border-radius`, so the backing card is rounded to match without
   restating it. */
/* Retargeted from `.composer-frame`, where it was dead code: a later rule in
   this branch resets the holder to transparent, so the white card and its
   offset backing had stopped rendering entirely. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-shell {
  background: var(--surface-panel);
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--border-subtle);
  border-radius: var(--fn-r-xl);
  box-shadow:
    var(--studio-backing-offset) var(--studio-backing-offset) 0 0 var(--studio-backing-fill),
    var(--studio-backing-offset) var(--studio-backing-offset) 0 var(--studio-edge) var(--border-subtle);
}

/* The field itself draws nothing — the composer's edge is the input's edge. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-frame :is(textarea, input) {
  background: transparent;
  border: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* ---- Icons: 线 + 面, in the glyph itself --------------------------------
   lucide draws `stroke="currentColor" fill="none"`, so both halves are set
   here: the line goes black and heavy, and the *closed* primitives take a solid
   yellow fill. That is the reference's construction exactly — its magnifier is
   a filled disc with a stroked handle, its toggle a filled rounded square with
   a stroked bar.

   Only closed primitives are filled. lucide's open paths — arrows, chevrons,
   waveforms — become blobs if filled, and `path` is also how it draws genuinely
   closed shapes like documents and folders, so there is no selector that
   separates the two. Document-shaped icons therefore stay unfilled here and
   need a purpose-drawn SVG to match the reference; noted in the spec. */
/* 2.1, down from 2.5. One value for the whole set on purpose: every chrome
   glyph in the frame comes through here — the sidebar's magnifier and panel
   toggle, the header's panel float, the composer's clip, shield, gauge,
   sparkle, chevrons and mic, the message actions — so thinning some and not
   others would put two line weights in one icon set. The two glyphs that are
   not lucide (the comic `+` and the reasoning infinity) carry their own
   weights and are unaffected. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] svg.lucide {
  stroke: var(--border-subtle);
  stroke-width: 2.1;
  stroke-linecap: round;
  stroke-linejoin: round;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] svg.lucide :is(circle, rect, ellipse, polygon) {
  fill: var(--accent);
}

/* ---- Studio palettes -------------------------------------------------- */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"][data-theme-preset="studio-citrus"] {
  --studio-sidebar-fill: #ffd426;
  --studio-panel-fill: #ffe98a;
  --studio-category-fill: #18c3f0;
  /* The thinking ink and the backing card follow the palette, or all four
     would carry Grape's violet. */
  --studio-thinking-ink: #6b5a00;
  --studio-backing-fill: #ffe98a;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"][data-theme-preset="studio-cobalt"] {
  --studio-sidebar-fill: #6b8fe8;
  --studio-panel-fill: #b9cef7;
  --studio-category-fill: #12b76a;
  --studio-thinking-ink: #34497a;
  --studio-backing-fill: #b9cef7;
}

/* Dark: the columns become panels rather than colour fields, because a
   saturated column would be the only lit thing in the room and would read as an
   error state. The outline carries the structure here instead. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"][data-theme-preset="studio-ink"] {
  --studio-sidebar-fill: #1c1c22;
  --studio-panel-fill: #16161c;
  --studio-category-fill: #4ecda4;
  /* Dark: the ink has to lighten, and the backing card cannot be a tint of the
     canvas or it would be invisible behind a card outlined in white. */
  --studio-thinking-ink: #b9aee0;
  --studio-backing-fill: #3a3a48;
}

/* Tier 2 — the composer's own controls are line-only in the reference, no fill
   and no container. They have to be excluded explicitly: `.composer-upload` is
   an icon button too, and a blanket rule flattens the tiers into one. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.composer-tools, .composer-actions) :is(button, .text-control),
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-upload {
  background: transparent;
  border: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
  color: var(--text-primary);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.composer-tools, .composer-actions) svg.lucide :is(circle, rect, ellipse, polygon),
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-upload svg.lucide :is(circle, rect, ellipse, polygon) {
  fill: none;
}

/* Tier 3 — status glyphs stay muted and unfilled: they report, they do not act. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.reasoning-header, .run-status-line, .tool-status) svg.lucide {
  stroke: var(--text-muted);
  stroke-width: 2;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.reasoning-header, .run-status-line, .tool-status) svg.lucide :is(circle, rect, ellipse, polygon) {
  fill: none;
}

/* ---- The two containers ------------------------------------------------
   Send: a yellow disc with a heavy black edge. Its glyph goes solid black —
   yellow on yellow would disappear, which is why the fill rule above has to be
   turned off in here rather than inherited. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .send-button {
  background: var(--accent);
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--border-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-full);
  color: var(--text-inverse);
  box-shadow: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .send-button:hover:not(:disabled) {
  background: var(--accent-hover);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .send-button svg.lucide {
  stroke: var(--text-inverse);
  fill: var(--text-inverse);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .send-button svg.lucide :is(circle, rect, ellipse, polygon) {
  fill: var(--text-inverse);
}

/* File-type badge: a squircle in the category colour, holding the glyph's own
   yellow+black pictogram. The only place a non-yellow fill appears, and the
   only place the fill means "what kind of thing is this" rather than "this is
   the primary action". */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .tool-card .tool-icon,
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .utility-card .output-file-icon {
  background: var(--studio-category-fill);
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--border-strong);
  border-radius: var(--fn-r-xl);
}

/* ---- Input -------------------------------------------------------------
   The holder draws nothing at all — no edge, no fill, no shadow. The card the
   reader sees is the field inside it, which brings its own. An edge here as
   well is the box-inside-a-box this style keeps inviting. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-frame {
  border: 0;
  background: transparent;
  box-shadow: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-frame :is(textarea, input) {
  background: transparent;
  border: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* Comic conversation and the states beyond the reference's completed turn. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] {
  --st-content-inset: var(--space-6);
  --tool-file-link: var(--studio-thinking-ink);
  --tool-file-link-hover: var(--text-primary);
  letter-spacing: 0;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .preview-stack {
  container: comic-conversation / inline-size;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .frame-header {
  /* `min-height` struck: the row height is shared. The inline inset is this
     style's own — see the note in the 复古风 branch. */
  padding-inline: var(--st-content-inset);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .frame-header h2 {
  font-size: var(--st-t-title);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-title);
  font-weight: 600;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .timeline-list {
  gap: var(--space-6);
  padding: var(--space-4) var(--st-content-inset) var(--space-6);
  scroll-padding-block: var(--space-4);
  -webkit-mask-image: none;
  mask-image: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .assistant-lane {
  gap: var(--space-4);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] :is(.message-role, .assistant-turn-label) {
  color: var(--studio-thinking-ink);
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-sidebar {
  padding-inline: var(--space-4);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-sidebar .session-item-label {
  font-weight: 500;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-item-shell[data-active="true"] .session-item-label {
  font-weight: 600;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .session-group > h4 {
  letter-spacing: 0;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .reasoning-body {
  padding-inline-start: var(--space-4);
  border-inline-start: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--studio-thinking-ink);
}

/* The spec's row weight, on the block so both halves take it. This named the
   label alone — and since the token block above already gave this style a
   colour and a 700 weight, the label ended up at 600 from here and the body at
   700 from there: the split, inside one style.
   The size is deliberately absent: it is 14px for every style now. */
.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .reasoning-block {
  --reasoning-type-weight: 600;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .tool-card {
  --st-tool-tone: var(--studio-thinking-ink);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .tool-card[data-status="success"] {
  --st-tool-tone: var(--success);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .tool-card[data-status="awaiting_approval"] {
  --st-tool-tone: var(--warning);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .tool-card[data-status="error"] {
  --st-tool-tone: var(--danger);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .tool-card .tool-status-icon {
  color: var(--st-tool-tone);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .tool-card .tool-status-icon svg {
  stroke: currentColor;
  fill: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .tool-card :is(.tool-title, .tool-file-row-status) {
  font-weight: 500;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .tool-title-file {
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--studio-thinking-ink) 40%, transparent);
  text-underline-offset: var(--space-1);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-launch-card {
  min-width: 0;
  gap: var(--space-3);
  padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
  border-radius: var(--st-r-panel);
  background: var(--surface-panel);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-launch-card .artifact-inline-icon {
  width: var(--st-h-ctl);
  height: var(--st-h-ctl);
  flex: none;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-launch-card .artifact-inline-body strong {
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  font-size: var(--st-t-title);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-title);
  font-weight: 600;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-action-open {
  min-height: var(--st-h-row);
  padding: var(--st-pad-snug) var(--space-3);
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid var(--st-ink);
  background: var(--surface-panel);
  color: var(--text-primary);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-action-open:hover:not(:disabled) {
  background: var(--surface-inset);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .artifact-action-open:disabled {
  background: var(--surface-disabled);
  color: var(--text-muted);
  cursor: not-allowed;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-frame {
  gap: var(--space-3);
  padding: var(--space-3) var(--st-content-inset) var(--space-5);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-shell {
  gap: var(--space-3);
  padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-shell:focus-within {
  outline: var(--studio-edge-frame) solid var(--studio-thinking-ink);
  outline-offset: var(--space-1);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-frame .ui-textarea {
  min-height: calc(var(--st-lh-body) * 2);
  font-size: var(--st-t-body);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-body);
  font-weight: 500;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .prompt-shortcuts {
  gap: var(--space-2);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .prompt-shortcuts .ui-button {
  min-height: var(--st-h-chip);
  padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
  border: var(--studio-edge) solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--st-ink) 35%, transparent);
  border-radius: var(--st-r-row);
  background: transparent;
  font-size: var(--st-t-label);
  line-height: var(--st-lh-label);
  font-weight: 500;
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .prompt-shortcuts .ui-button:hover {
  border-color: var(--st-ink);
  background: var(--surface-panel);
}

.preview-frame[data-style-preset="studio"] .composer-tools {
